{"timestamp": "2026-01-15 03:03:58", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e545f5eb-3c55-41ff-8ac7-e6294c2eb561', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md",\n  "project_name": "Famous Mustards Achievements"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_016bvkVxjMHGV1HjA3qaggtX', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_016bvkVxjMHGV1HjA3qaggtX', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Famous Mustards Achievements - Requirements\\n\\n## Project Vision\\nAdd 10 new achievements from the category \\"famous mustards\\" to the existing achievement system in the ensemble project.\\n\\n## Objectives\\n1. Create a new achievement category `FAMOUS_MUSTARDS` in the existing system\\n2. Design 10 thematic achievements based on famous mustard varieties and mustard-related concepts\\n3. Integrate these achievements into the existing achievement tracking system\\n4. Ensure achievements follow the established pattern and quality standards\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n- Add `FAMOUS_MUSTARDS` to the `AchievementCategory` enum\\n- Create 10 new achievement definitions with:\\n  - Unique IDs related to mustard themes\\n  - Creative names referencing famous mustard types/brands\\n  - Descriptive text that ties mustard concepts to agent behaviors\\n  - Appropriate rarity levels (mix of common to legendary)\\n  - Relevant icons (mustard/food related emojis)\\n  - Point values consistent with existing system\\n  - Trigger conditions that make sense in context\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- Modifying the achievement tracking logic or database schema\\n- Changing the UI components for displaying achievements\\n- Adding achievements in other categories\\n- Modifying existing achievements\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n### Functional Requirements\\n\\n**R1: Category Addition**\\n- Add `FAMOUS_MUSTARDS = \\"famous_mustards\\"` to the AchievementCategory enum\\n\\n**R2: Achievement Definitions**\\n- Create exactly 10 new Achievement objects\\n- Each must reference famous mustard varieties (Dijon, Yellow, Honey Mustard, Spicy Brown, etc.)\\n- Names should be creative but clearly mustard-themed\\n- Descriptions should cleverly relate mustard characteristics to agent behaviors\\n- Must follow existing achievement patterns and structure\\n\\n**R3: Quality Standards**\\n- Achievements should have varied rarity levels (suggested: 3-4 common, 3-4 uncommon, 2-3 rare, 0-1 epic/legendary)\\n- Point values should align with rarity (10-15 common, 20-35 uncommon, 40-60 rare, 75+ epic/legendary)\\n- Icons should be food/mustard related emojis\\n- Trigger conditions should be realistic and achievable\\n\\n### Technical Requirements\\n\\n**T1: Code Integration**\\n- Achievements must be added to the existing ACHIEVEMENTS list in achievements.py\\n- Follow existing code structure and formatting\\n- No breaking changes to existing functionality\\n\\n**T2: Naming Conventions**\\n- Achievement IDs should follow snake_case pattern\\n- IDs should be descriptive and mustard-themed\\n- Names should be title case and engaging\\n\\n### Constraints\\n\\n**C1: System Compatibility**\\n- Must work with existing achievement tracking system\\n- Cannot modify database schema or core tracking logic\\n- Must use existing trigger condition patterns\\n\\n**C2: Theme Consistency**\\n- All achievements must be mustard-themed\\n- Should maintain the humorous, gaming-style tone of existing achievements\\n- Must fit naturally with the existing achievement ecosystem\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. \\u2705 10 new achievements added to the system\\n2. \\u2705 All achievements are mustard-themed and creative\\n3. \\u2705 Code integrates cleanly without breaking existing functionality\\n4. \\u2705 Achievements follow established patterns for structure and quality\\n5. \\u2705 Mixed rarity distribution provides progression incentives\\n6. \\u2705 Trigger conditions are realistic and varied\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n- The existing achievement system architecture will not change\\n- The mustard theme should be applied broadly (types, brands, characteristics, uses)\\n- Achievements should maintain the existing humorous and gaming-inspired tone\\n- Standard software development practices will be followed\\n\\n## Technical Context\\n\\nThe system already has these achievement categories:\\n- SKA, BRASS_BAND, DRUM_CORPS, GUITAR_HERO (music-themed)\\n- PRODUCTIVITY, COMEDY, MILESTONE, STREAK, META (behavior-themed)\\n\\nThe new FAMOUS_MUSTARDS category will join this list and achievements will be added to the main ACHIEVEMENTS list in `/src/runtime/agents/achievements.py`."}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_018a8NuXaF9RnJsNKdhxYjBk', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': '# Famous Mustards Achievements - Architecture Proposal\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\n### High-Level Design\nThis feature adds a new achievement category to the existing ensemble project\'s achievement system. The architecture follows the **Extension Pattern** - adding new data while preserving existing system behavior and structure.\n\n**Architecture Pattern**: Data Extension Pattern\n- Minimal code changes to existing system\n- Additive approach (no modifications to existing achievements or core logic)\n- Maintains backward compatibility and system stability\n\n**Rationale**: Since this is purely additive content (new achievements in a new category), the safest and most maintainable approach is to extend the existing data structures without touching the achievement tracking logic, UI components, or database schema.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n### Existing Stack (No Changes)\n- **Language**: Python (existing achievement system)\n- **Data Structure**: Python enums and dataclasses/objects\n- **Storage**: Existing achievement tracking system (presumably database-backed)\n\n### No Additional Dependencies Required\nThis feature requires no new libraries, frameworks, or external dependencies. It works entirely within the existing achievement system infrastructure.\n\n**Why No New Dependencies**: \n- Keeps system lightweight and maintainable\n- Reduces deployment complexity\n- Minimizes security and compatibility risks\n- Follows the project\'s established patterns\n\n## System Components\n\n### Component Breakdown\n\n#### 1. AchievementCategory Enum Extension\n**Location**: `/src/runtime/agents/achievements.py`\n**Responsibility**: Define the new FAMOUS_MUSTARDS category\n**Changes**: Add single enum value\n**Interaction**: Referenced by new achievement definitions\n\n#### 2. Achievement Definitions\n**Location**: `/src/runtime/agents/achievements.py` (ACHIEVEMENTS list)\n**Responsibility**: Define the 10 mustard-themed achievements\n**Changes**: Add 10 new Achievement objects to existing list\n**Interaction**: Consumed by existing achievement tracking system\n\n#### 3. Existing Components (No Changes)\n- **Achievement Tracking Logic**: Remains unchanged\n- **UI Components**: Remains unchanged  \n- **Database Schema**: Remains unchanged\n- **API Endpoints**: Remains unchanged\n\n### Data Flow\n```\nNew Achievement Definitions\n    ↓\nAdded to ACHIEVEMENTS List\n    ↓\nExisting Achievement System\n    ↓\nExisting Tracking Logic\n    ↓\nExisting UI Display\n```\n\n## File/Directory Structure\n\n### Modified Files\n```\n/src/runtime/agents/\n├── achievements.py          # ONLY file requiring changes\n│   ├── AchievementCategory  # Add FAMOUS_MUSTARDS enum value\n│   └── ACHIEVEMENTS        # Add 10 new Achievement objects\n```\n\n### No New Files Required\nAll changes occur within the existing achievements.py file structure.\n\n## Data Model\n\n### Achievement Category Extension\n```python\nclass AchievementCategory(Enum):\n    # ... existing categories ...\n    FAMOUS_MUSTARDS = "famous_mustards"  # NEW\n```\n\n### Achievement Object Structure (Existing Pattern)\n```python\nAchievement(\n    id="mustard_themed_id",           # snake_case, mustard-themed\n    category=AchievementCategory.FAMOUS_MUSTARDS,\n    name="Creative Mustard Name",      # Title case, engaging\n    description="Mustard behavior description",\n    icon="🌭",                        # Food/mustard emoji\n    rarity=AchievementRarity.COMMON,   # Varied distribution\n    points=15,                         # Aligned with rarity\n    trigger_conditions={}              # Existing pattern triggers\n)\n```\n\n### Rarity Distribution Strategy\n- **3-4 Common** (10-15 points): Basic mustard types (Yellow, Classic)\n- **3-4 Uncommon** (20-35 points): Popular varieties (Honey, Spicy Brown)\n- **2-3 Rare** (40-60 points): Gourmet types (Dijon, Whole Grain)\n- **0-1 Epic/Legendary** (75+ points): Premium/specialty mustards\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n### Phase 1: Category Definition\n1. Add FAMOUS_MUSTARDS to AchievementCategory enum\n2. Test enum integration with existing system\n\n### Phase 2: Achievement Design\n1. Research famous mustard varieties and characteristics\n2. Design 10 achievements mapping mustard traits to agent behaviors\n3. Assign appropriate rarity levels and point values\n4. Select thematic emojis and create engaging descriptions\n\n### Phase 3: Integration\n1. Add all 10 achievements to ACHIEVEMENTS list\n2. Verify no syntax errors or conflicts\n3. Test achievement system functionality\n\n### Phase 4: Validation\n1. Confirm achievements appear in system\n2. Verify trigger conditions work as expected\n3. Test rarity distribution and point allocation\n\n## Achievement Design Philosophy\n\n### Mustard-to-Behavior Mapping Strategy\n- **Yellow Mustard**: Basic/foundational behaviors\n- **Dijon**: Sophisticated/refined actions  \n- **Honey Mustard**: Sweet/collaborative behaviors\n- **Spicy Brown**: Intense/high-energy actions\n- **Whole Grain**: Complex/detailed work\n- **Beer Mustard**: Social/team behaviors\n- **Horseradish**: Sharp/critical thinking\n- **Truffle Mustard**: Rare/exceptional achievements\n\n### Trigger Condition Patterns (Using Existing System)\n- Task completion counts\n- Collaboration metrics\n- Quality indicators\n- Time-based achievements\n- Behavioral consistency patterns\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Unit Testing Approach\n- **Enum Validation**: Test FAMOUS_MUSTARDS category integration\n- **Object Validation**: Verify all 10 achievements have required fields\n- **Data Integrity**: Ensure no duplicate IDs or conflicting values\n\n### Integration Testing Approach\n- **System Integration**: Confirm achievements appear in tracking system\n- **UI Integration**: Verify achievements display correctly (existing UI)\n- **Trigger Testing**: Test sample trigger conditions work\n\n### Validation Criteria\n- [ ] All achievements have unique, themed IDs\n- [ ] Rarity distribution matches specification (3-4 common, etc.)\n- [ ] Point values align with rarity levels\n- [ ] Icons are food/mustard themed emojis\n- [ ] No breaking changes to existing functionality\n\n## Deployment Strategy\n\n### Environment Configuration\n**No environment changes required** - this is pure code addition.\n\n### Deployment Steps\n1. **Code Review**: Review achievement definitions for quality and theme consistency\n2. **Testing**: Run existing test suite to ensure no regressions\n3. **Deployment**: Standard code deployment (no special requirements)\n4. **Verification**: Confirm achievements appear in production system\n\n### CI/CD Considerations\n- Existing CI/CD pipeline handles this automatically\n- No additional deployment steps or configurations needed\n- Standard code review and testing processes apply\n\n## Risk Analysis\n\n### Risks and Mitigations\n\n#### Risk 1: ID Conflicts\n**Risk**: New achievement IDs conflict with existing ones\n**Likelihood**: Low\n**Mitigation**: Careful ID selection with mustard prefixes (e.g., "mustard_", "dijon_")\n\n#### Risk 2: Theme Consistency\n**Risk**: Achievements don\'t fit the established tone\n**Likelihood**: Medium\n**Mitigation**: Review existing achievements for tone/style consistency before writing\n\n#### Risk 3: Rarity Balance\n**Risk**: Point values or rarity distribution disrupts game balance\n**Likelihood**: Low\n**Mitigation**: Follow established patterns from existing achievements\n\n#### Risk 4: Trigger Condition Complexity\n**Risk**: Trigger conditions are too hard/easy to achieve\n**Likelihood**: Medium\n**Mitigation**: Analyze existing trigger patterns and align new conditions accordingly\n\n### Minimal Risk Profile\nThis feature has a very low risk profile because:\n- No changes to existing code logic\n- No database schema modifications\n- No UI component changes\n- Pure additive content approach\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Alternative 1: New Achievement System\n**Approach**: Build separate mustard achievement system\n**Rejected Because**: \n- Massive over-engineering for simple content addition\n- Would duplicate existing infrastructure\n- High complexity and maintenance burden\n\n### Alternative 2: Generic Food Category\n**Approach**: Create broader "FOOD" category with mustards as subset\n**Rejected Because**: \n- Requirements specifically request mustard-focused category\n- Would dilute thematic consistency\n- Doesn\'t match existing specific category pattern\n\n### Alternative 3: Modify Existing Categories\n**Approach**: Add mustard achievements to existing categories\n**Rejected Because**: \n- Violates theme consistency of existing categories\n- Requirements specifically request new FAMOUS_MUSTARDS category\n- Would confuse the achievement organization system\n\n### Chosen Approach Advantages\n- **Minimal Risk**: Only additive changes\n- **Theme Focused**: Dedicated mustard category as requested\n- **Maintainable**: Follows established patterns\n- **Scalable**: Easy to add more achievements later\n\n## Open Questions for User Review\n\n### Major Architectural Decisions\n1. **Rarity Distribution**: Proposed 3-4 common, 3-4 uncommon, 2-3 rare, 0-1 epic. Acceptable?\n2. **Trigger Complexity**: Should achievements use simple triggers (task counts) or complex behavioral patterns?\n3. **Point Scaling**: Should points align with existing achievement categories or have mustard-specific scaling?\n\n### Theme Direction Questions\n1. **Humor Level**: How humorous should mustard-behavior connections be?\n2. **Brand References**: OK to reference real mustard brands (Grey Poupon, French\'s) or stick to generic types?\n3. **Culinary Detail**: How deep should mustard knowledge go (basic varieties vs. gourmet details)?\n\n### Implementation Preferences\n1. **Achievement Names**: Prefer puns/wordplay or straightforward descriptive names?\n2. **Icon Selection**: Mustard jar emojis, food emojis, or broader food-related icons?\n\n## Next Steps\n\n1. **User Approval**: Review and approve this architecture proposal\n2. **Content Creation**: Design specific achievement content based on approved approach\n3. **Implementation**: Code the 10 achievements following this architectural plan\n4. **Testing**: Validate integration and functionality\n5. **Deployment**: Release to production environment\n\n## Success Metrics\n\n- ✅ Zero breaking changes to existing system\n- ✅ All 10 achievements integrate cleanly\n- ✅ Theme consistency maintained throughout\n- ✅ Rarity distribution provides proper progression\n- ✅ Code follows existing patterns and conventions\n- ✅ No additional dependencies or complexity introduced\n\n---\n\n**Architecture Confidence Level**: High\n**Implementation Complexity**: Low  \n**Estimated Development Time**: 2-4 hours for content creation\n**Risk Level**: Very Low'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_018a8NuXaF9RnJsNKdhxYjBk', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_030358.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_030358.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.6\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The proposed architecture successfully addresses the core requirements by providing a nuanced approach to file synchronization. The design balances performance, reliability, and simplicity while adher...\n- Successfully analyzed file pane stability requirements and designed a comprehensive architecture addressing all constraints. The intelligent polling approach with file system monitoring provides the r...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create