{"timestamp": "2026-01-15 03:04:08", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-fe186195-8b24-419d-ac26-892573188c73', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md",\n  "project_name": "Katamari Damacy Achievements"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_019MD43oFrWoFF4DLC1N93DX', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_019MD43oFrWoFF4DLC1N93DX', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Katamari Damacy Achievements - Requirements Document\\n\\n## Project Overview\\n\\n**Project Name:** Katamari Damacy Achievements  \\n**Project ID:** 12a0e2a7  \\n**Created:** 2026-01-15  \\n**Executive Director:** Orchestrating implementation\\n\\n## Vision\\n\\nAdd 10 new achievements from the \\"Katamari Damacy\\" category to the existing Ensemble achievements system. Katamari Damacy is a beloved Japanese video game where players roll a katamari (magic ball) around to collect objects and grow larger, with quirky humor and delightful chaos.\\n\\n## Background: Katamari Damacy\\n\\nKatamari Damacy (2004) is a unique puzzle-action video game created by Keita Takahashi. Key themes:\\n- **Rolling Ball Mechanics**: Start small, roll up objects to grow larger\\n- **Absurd Scale**: Begin picking up thumbtacks, end up rolling up buildings and mountains\\n- **Whimsical Humor**: Eccentric King of All Cosmos, bizarre storylines, surreal physics\\n- **Size Progression**: Measured in centimeters/meters, achieving specific size targets\\n- **Collection Gameplay**: Pick up everything from paper clips to skyscrapers\\n- **Time Limits**: Many levels have strict time constraints\\n- **Colorful Chaos**: Vibrant, playful aesthetics with Japanese quirkiness\\n\\n## Objectives\\n\\n1. **Add New Category**: Create `KATAMARI` achievement category in the enum\\n2. **Achievement Content**: Add exactly 10 Katamari Damacy themed achievements\\n3. **UI Updates**: Add katamari category to filter dropdown in AchievementsDashboard\\n4. **Maintain Consistency**: Follow existing achievement patterns for rarity, triggers, and points\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n\\n**Backend Changes** (`src/runtime/agents/achievements.py`):\\n- Add `KATAMARI = \\"katamari\\"` to `AchievementCategory` enum\\n- Add 10 new `Achievement` objects to the `ACHIEVEMENTS` list\\n- Include variety of rarities (common through legendary)\\n\\n**Frontend Changes** (`src/field/ensemble_ui/frontend/src/components/AchievementsDashboard.jsx`):\\n- Add katamari category to filter dropdown with appropriate icon\\n- Add katamari to `getCategoryBadge` function\\n- Consider adding Katamari facts to fun facts section\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- New API endpoints (using existing endpoints)\\n- Database schema changes (existing schema supports new category)\\n- New UI components beyond filter updates\\n\\n## Achievement Definitions\\n\\n### Theme Guidelines\\nKatamari achievements should reference:\\n- Rolling mechanics (rolling up objects, growing bigger)\\n- Size progression (centimeters to kilometers)  \\n- Collection themes (picking up various items)\\n- Time constraints and urgency\\n- The King of All Cosmos and royal family\\n- Absurd scale jumps (tiny to cosmic)\\n- Japanese terminology (katamari, cousin characters)\\n\\n### Proposed Achievements (Exactly 10)\\n\\n| ID | Name | Description | Rarity | Points | Icon |\\n|----|------|-------------|--------|--------|------|\\n| `katamari_start` | We Love Katamari | Completed your first task - starting to roll! | COMMON | 10 | \\ud83c\\udf1f |\\n| `royal_rainbow` | Royal Rainbow | Successfully completed 5 different types of tasks | UNCOMMON | 25 | \\ud83c\\udf08 |\\n| `cosmic_collection` | Cosmic Collection | Gathered 25+ files/resources in a single session | RARE | 45 | \\ud83c\\udf0c |\\n| `king_of_cosmos` | King of All Cosmos | Orchestrated 10+ sub-agents like the King commanding cousins | EPIC | 80 | \\ud83d\\udc51 |\\n| `size_matters` | We Love Katamari - Size Matters | Grew from small task to handling 100+ operations | RARE | 50 | \\ud83d\\udccf |\\n| `sticky_situation` | Sticky Situation | Recovered from 3 failures and succeeded (things stick to the katamari) | UNCOMMON | 30 | \\ud83c\\udf6f |\\n| `cousin_collaboration` | Cousin Collaboration | Multiple agents working together successfully | UNCOMMON | 35 | \\ud83d\\udc65 |\\n| `time_attack` | Katamari Time Attack | Completed task under extreme time pressure (<30 seconds) | RARE | 55 | \\u23f0 |\\n| `everything_collector` | I Rolled Up Everything | Completed 50 total tasks - you\'ve rolled up the whole universe! | LEGENDARY | 150 | \\ud83c\\udf0d |\\n| `prince_upgrade` | Prince of All Cosmos | Achieved 95%+ success rate across 20+ tasks | LEGENDARY | 200 | \\ud83e\\udd34 |\\n\\n### Achievement Implementation Pattern\\n\\n```python\\n# Example achievement definition pattern\\nAchievement(\\n    id=\\"katamari_start\\",\\n    name=\\"We Love Katamari\\",\\n    description=\\"Completed your first task - starting to roll!\\",\\n    category=AchievementCategory.KATAMARI,\\n    rarity=AchievementRarity.COMMON,\\n    icon=\\"\\ud83c\\udf1f\\",\\n    points=10,\\n    agent_classes=[\\"*\\"],\\n    trigger_condition={\\"event\\": \\"first_execution\\"}\\n)\\n```\\n\\n## Technical Constraints\\n\\n1. **Existing Patterns**: Must follow existing achievement definition pattern in achievements.py\\n2. **Category Enum**: Add to existing `AchievementCategory` enum\\n3. **Frontend Consistency**: Match existing category badge/filter patterns\\n4. **Trigger Conditions**: Use existing trigger condition types where possible\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. \\u2705 `KATAMARI` category added to `AchievementCategory` enum\\n2. \\u2705 Exactly 10 Katamari achievements added to `ACHIEVEMENTS` list\\n3. \\u2705 Frontend filter dropdown includes katamari with appropriate icon\\n4. \\u2705 `getCategoryBadge` function handles katamari category\\n5. \\u2705 All existing tests continue to pass\\n6. \\u2705 Achievements unlock correctly when conditions are met\\n\\n## Implementation Notes\\n\\n### Files to Modify\\n- **Backend**: `src/runtime/agents/achievements.py`\\n- **Frontend**: `src/field/ensemble_ui/frontend/src/components/AchievementsDashboard.jsx`\\n\\n### Icon Choice\\nUsing \\ud83c\\udf1f as primary katamari icon since actual katamari ball isn\'t available in standard emoji sets.\\n\\n### Testing\\n- Run existing achievement tests to ensure no regressions\\n- Manually verify katamari filter works in UI\\n- Verify at least one achievement can be triggered\\n\\n## Assumptions Made\\n\\n1. **Icon Choice**: Using \\ud83c\\udf1f, \\ud83d\\udc51, \\ud83c\\udf08, \\ud83c\\udf0c and other playful emojis to capture Katamari\'s whimsical aesthetic\\n2. **Trigger Conditions**: Reusing existing trigger types (first_execution, consecutive_successes, etc.)\\n3. **Points Balance**: Following existing points range (10-200) based on rarity\\n4. **Theme Interpretation**: Focusing on universal Katamari themes (rolling, collecting, growing) rather than obscure game references\\n\\n## Fun Facts for UI (Optional Enhancement)\\n\\nConsider adding to the fun facts section:\\n> **Did you know?** Katamari Damacy was created by Keita Takahashi in 2004 as an anti-game that rejected traditional video game violence. Players roll a magical adhesive ball called a \\"katamari\\" to pick up objects, starting with thumbtacks and paperclips and eventually collecting buildings, islands, and even stars! The King of All Cosmos destroyed all the stars (accidentally, of course) and tasks the Prince with rolling up replacements. The game\'s absurd humor and unique physics made it a cult classic that spawned multiple sequels. Na na na na na na na na Katamari Damacy! \\ud83c\\udf1f\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Status:** Requirements Complete - Ready for Implementation  \\n**Next Step:** Delegate to Development Manager for implementation"}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.6\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The proposed architecture successfully addresses the core requirements by providing a nuanced approach to file synchronization. The design balances performance, reliability, and simplicity while adher...\n- Successfully analyzed file pane stability requirements and designed a comprehensive architecture addressing all constraints. The intelligent polling approach with file system monitoring provides the r...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branc